AI-based predictions for drug targets for antibiotic resistance proteins- multidrug efflux pumps in Pseudomonas aeruginosa 

Abstract 

Multidrug efflux pumps are major contributors to antibiotic resistance in Pseudomonas aeruginosa, making their inhibition an important strategy for improving 
antimicrobial efficacy. MexB, the inner-membrane component of the MexAB-OprM resistance-nodulation-division (RND) efflux system, contains structurally distinct
drug-binding regions that accommodate chemically diverse compounds (Murakami et al., 2006; Du et al., 2015).The present study employed a structure-based computational 
approach to characterize the drug-binding pocket (DBP) of MexB and identify potential ligand-binding candidates. The MexB binding-state structure was prepared and analyzed
using structural alignment and pocket-prediction methods, with P2Rank used to identify candidate ligand-binding regions (Krivák and Hoksza, 2018). 
A docking grid was subsequently defined around the selected deep binding pocket, and 30 ChEMBL-derived ligands were screened using AutoDock Vina (Eberhardt et al., 2021).  
The five highest-scoring predicted ligands showed docking affinities ranging from −10.44 to −12.90 kcal/mol. Interaction analysis identified six recurrent DBP residues 
PHE178, VAL139, TYR327, PHE573, PHE610, and PHE628 which were used as a structural contact signature for further validation. All five predicted top ligands showed heavy-atom
distances 
of ≤4 Å from all six core residues. To assess whether the selected pocket was also compatible with experimentally characterized compounds, four reference ligands were independently docked.
Three of the four reference compounds contacted five or six of the six core residues, while one contacted four residues. The aromatic-rich nature of the identified region is 
consistent withprevious structural studies describing a phenylalanine-rich drug-binding environment within the MexB distal pocket (Nakashima et al., 2013). Overall, 
the results provide computational evidence supporting the selected MexB DBP as a recurrent ligand-interaction region and identify candidate compounds for further investigation. 
However, docking scores and distance-based contacts represent computational predictions and do not establish experimental binding affinity or efflux-inhibition activity; 
biochemical, cellular, molecular-dynamics, or other experimental validation would therefore be required to confirm the predicted interactions (Eberhardt et al., 2021). 
